    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d887">

                <pb facs="0832" n="820" sameAs="frus:frus1944v05/pg820"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.20 Defense(M)Turkey/927: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Turkey</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Steinhardt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Ankara</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-02-23T14:00:00+02:00">February 23,
                            1944—2 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 10:17 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>319. Department’s 135 February 21, 6 p.m. As reported in my 284 February 18, 1
                    p.m., I have already made a vigorous protest in writing. In subsequent
                    conversation with the Secretary General of the Foreign Office,<anchor n="12"
                        xml:id="d887-f1"/> he informed me that immediately upon receipt of my
                    protest the Foreign Office had given instructions that the movement of chrome
                    for British account be materially increased. Although he gave me no specific
                    assurance of a decrease in the movement of chrome for German account I gained
                    the impression that this will follow.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Steinhardt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
